# /build/ is generated output, never a source. status-dashboard-generate.sh renders
# build/dashboard.html from status-generate.sh --json, doc-freshness.sh and git at render
# time; committing it would turn a VIEW of the numbers into a second COPY of
# them, which is what lint T2 exists to prevent — and a stale chart is read as
# current far longer than stale prose is.
#
# ANCHORED, and that leading slash is load-bearing. A bare `build/` matches at
# every depth, and plugins/atlas keeps its SOURCE in a directory of that name —
# so the rule written for one generated dashboard was hiding 9 of the atlas
# plugin's 13 files, every one of them code. Measured 2026-09-03: build.py,
# atlas.js, atlas_css.py, check.mjs and namespace.py were all invisible to git
# while `git status` read clean, which is the failure mode a .gitignore rule is
# worst at showing you. The anchor keeps the dashboard ignored and nothing else.
/build/

# Python bytecode. Previously covered by accident: the unanchored `build/` above
# was hiding plugins/atlas/skills/atlas/build/__pycache__ along with all the
# source beside it, so anchoring that rule exposed these for the first time. They
# are generated, machine-specific and version-stamped (cpython-314), so they are
# named explicitly now rather than left to a rule that was never about them.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# macOS Finder metadata. Untracked-but-present on every directory the
# operator has opened in Finder; it is noise in `git status`, not content.
.DS_Store

# Archify's rendered example diagrams. `archify examples` runs
# vendor/archify/scripts/render-examples.mjs, whose table at lines 13-17 names
# these five outputs and whose outputRoot defaults to examples/ — so they land
# beside the .json inputs that produced them, in the same directory git watches.
#
# THE JSON IS THE SOURCE AND SAYS SO ITSELF: each input carries an "output" key
# naming its own HTML, so the render is fully derivable and nothing tracked
# consumes the result — the only two references to each file are the input that
# declares it and the renderer that writes it. Committing them would be lint T2's
# second copy at 3.5 MB, and a stale diagram is read as current the same way a
# stale chart is.
#
# Anchored, like /build/ above, and scoped to the one directory the renderer
# writes into: vendor/archify/assets/template.html is a SOURCE template and must
# stay tracked.
/vendor/archify/examples/*.html

# Claude Code's isolated agent worktrees. Nested git checkouts, never payload (deploy
# reads only .claude/inject.d/), but untracked they made deploy's preflight refuse the
# main checkout as dirty whenever a session ran agents with isolation: worktree.
/.claude/worktrees/

# The context baseline lives in the user's state directory (harbor#1275); this keeps a stray copy at the root of a
# harbor-core clone from being staged. The dev checkout's tracked seed is unaffected: ignore rules never untrack.
/registries/context-baseline.tsv

# docs/index.html is BUILT on demand, never committed (harbor#1197): `sh bin/doc-index-generate.sh --apply` (~14 s). Committed, it
# was touched by 126 of 317 first-parent main commits in 30 days, and every pair of open PRs conflicted on it; merge-stack
# refuses an HTML conflict, so each one stopped for a person. The export builds it into the core snapshot (bin/deploy-push.sh).
/docs/index.html
